@@s001 Examples: fold and monoid in Scala
@@s002 <span>Week 9 · examples</span>
@@s003 Examples: fold and monoid in Scala
@@s004 The code of the <a href="../teoria/s09-foldable.html">foldable page</a>: fold with <code>foldLeft</code> and <code>foldRight</code>, and the reduction with a monoid done in two steps (first <code>map</code>, then <code>concatenate</code>). Checked with Scala 3.8.4 and the course options. The real <code>foldMap</code> is the course exercise: it is not here.
@@s005 The whole file
@@s006 Run it with <code>scala-cli run Foldable.scala</code>. It uses two different monoids on the same type of list: the maximum of integers and the concatenation of strings.
@@s007 Foldable.scala
@@s008 <code>concatenate</code> is a fold on the monoid: it starts from the neutral element <code>m.empty</code> and combines with <code>m.combine</code>. The type <code>A</code> is generic: it works for any monoid.
@@s009 <code>foldLeft(0)</code> starts from 0 and reduces from the left. The lambda <code>(acc, p) =&gt; acc + p.length</code> takes the accumulator and the element, and returns the new accumulator. Here the sum of the lengths: 6 + 4 + 3 + 6 = 19.
@@s010 <code>foldRight</code> does the same work from the right. For the sum the result is the same (19), because the sum is a monoid: the order does not matter.
@@s011 <code>map</code> turns each word into its length: List(6, 4, 3, 6). Then <code>concatenate</code> reduces them with the maximum: 6. This is the foldMap in two steps.
@@s012 Same pattern with another monoid: <code>take(1)</code> takes the first letter, and the concatenation joins them into <b>mfmr</b>. The type B is String, different from the type of the words.
@@s013 Empty list: <code>concatenate</code> returns the neutral element of <code>maxMonoid</code>, <code>Int.MinValue</code>.
@@s014 I split the list into two halves, reduce them separately and combine them with <code>+</code> (string concatenation). It is the parallel grouping from the theory: the result is "ab" + "cd".
@@s015 Reduction in one step of the whole list: "abcd". Same result as the example above, by associativity.
@@s016 The output
@@s017 Output of the run
@@s018 Sum of the lengths with <code>foldLeft</code> and <code>foldRight</code>: equal.
@@s019 Maximum of the lengths, then concatenation of the first letters.
@@s020 Neutral element of the maximum on an empty list.
@@s021 Two groupings of "a", "b", "c", "d": same result, "abcd".
@@s022 To remember
@@s023 Sentence for the exam
@@s024 A fold over a monoid does not depend on the order of the elements, nor on how they are grouped.
@@s025 Practical rule
@@s026 To compute a foldMap by hand: <code>map</code> on the list with the function, then <code>concatenate</code> with the monoid of the target type.
